X:1
T:Usagi to Kame (兎と亀)
T:The Rabbit and the Turtle
C:Benjirō Nōsho (music); Wasaburō Ishihara (Japanese words); Grace Hazard Conkling (1922 English version)
O:Japan
R:children's song (school song)
S:Florence Hudson Botsford (ed.), Folk Songs of Many Peoples, vol. 2 (New York: The Woman's Press, 1922), "The Rabbit and the Turtle," pp. 442-443, English version by Grace Hazard Conkling; University of Rochester Sibley Music Library, public domain
N:A Japanese school song of 1901, not a folk song: music by Benjirō Nōsho and words by Wasaburō Ishihara, written for young children in the Meiji school-song movement. The fable of the hare and the tortoise as a dialogue: here the rabbit teases the slow turtle and challenges it to a race. Sing it in Grace Hazard Conkling's English version (1922); let one group be the rabbit and the class answer as the turtle, boastful against patient.
N:The Japanese words are printed in the source but are not yet set to the notes here; a Japanese speaker should set them. The book marks Allegro; the tempo is ours. Voice and a steady walking pulse; no koto, shamisen or taiko.
M:2/4
L:1/16
Q:1/4=104
